    "available_at": "2026-10-13T20:02:16.709373+00:00",
    "body": "Athletic apparel brand Alo has established a national fulfillment and distribution center in Southaven, Mississippi, investing nearly $176 million and planning more than 400 new jobs . The Mississippi Development Authority announced the project on Oct. 12, after work began in August.\nWhat’s Related\nThe company is moving into an existing building, where it will occupy up to 1.2 million square feet. Alo also has the option to add returns processing at the facility, although the announcement does not say whether or when that work will begin.\nThe center will serve customers across the country. Alo plans to fill the new jobs by 2027, according to the state announcement, and company co-president Norm Hullinger said additional hiring is expected during the holiday season .\nMississippi is providing assistance through its Mississippi Flexible Tax Incentive, known as MFLEX. DeSoto County and the city of Southaven are also assisting with the project.\nRELATED STORIES: Hitachi Energy Plans $528 Million Mississippi Transformer Plant | Amazon Expands Mississippi Presence with Last-Mile Facility in Saltillo\nShipping orders nationwide\nThe Southaven operation will handle both fulfillment and distribution for Alo’s apparel business. The announcement identifies it as a national center but does not specify whether it will serve online orders, stores, or both.\nMississippi Development Authority Executive Director PJ Waldrop mentioned transportation access and the local workforce as advantages of the location. The announcement did not provide details about warehouse equipment, automation , shipping volumes or delivery times.\nHiring extends into next year\nAlo’s staffing plans include more than 400 new positions, with Hullinger mentioning additional holiday hiring separately. The announcement did not give a number for seasonal positions or provide a breakdown of permanent job roles.\n“We’re proud to create more than 400 new jobs upon opening, and many more to come during the holiday season,” Hullinger said.\nThe state’s timetable places completion of hiring in 2027, even though operations are already underway. Alo’s option to add returns processing could bring another function to the building, but that remains a possible addition rather than an announced operating date.",
